From 97807cb5cf9fa07428fbbe873679e55053b7f67a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <claude@anthropic.com>
Date: Tue, 11 Aug 2026 08:03:01 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] P8-printing-module-image-and-config-revision

Printing Module, Phase 8 - revision request: 'Print Images & Product Print Configuration'
(6 points, all confirmed before implementation).

1. Upload/replace/migrate authorization flipped to system-admin-only (was tenant-only).
   New GET /api/print-images/{id}, both roles, tenant-scoped for a tenant caller - the only
   way to fetch an image now; no public static-file path exists any more.
2. Tenant folders now named after Tenant.Username (sanitized), not TenantId - confirmed.
3. Physical file names now the sanitized original client name, not a generated GUID.
4. Duplicate handling: POST is create-only (409 Conflict + existing image's metadata, never
   auto-replaces). Explicit replace is its own endpoint: PUT /api/print-images/{id} updates the
   same PrintImage row's content in place - same Id, so any product print configuration
   referencing it by ImageId is never left dangling by a replace, and there was no need to block
   replacing an in-use image after all.
5. FontSize CHECK constraints on both product print config tables relaxed from '> 0' to '>= 0'
   (Cpi already had no constraint). 0 is now a valid value for both.
6. ImagePath (bare string) replaced with ImageId (FK -> PrintImages.Id) on both
   MaticaProductPrintConfiguration and EvolisProductPrintConfiguration. Composer validates
   ImageId existence + tenant match, mirroring the existing RibbonTypeId check.

New: FileSystemNameSanitizer (tenant-folder and filename sanitization, Windows-reserved-name
guard, fails closed rather than silently inventing a name). One-time, idempotent, admin-triggered
POST /api/print-images/migrate-legacy-storage moves already-uploaded images (GUID name,
tenant-id folder) to the new scheme - not a background job, an explicit on-demand operational
endpoint, consistent with the standing 'no scheduled cleanup mechanism' decision.

This phase started from a large batch of uncommitted work already present in the working tree
(from an interrupted earlier session) rather than a blank slate. Every file was read end-to-end
against the confirmed design before being trusted, not assumed correct. Found and fixed during
that review, before anything was committed:

- Migration re-entrancy bug: the idempotency check recomputed the expected file name from
  scratch every run. A second run following a sanitization-collision-driven rename (e.g.
  'front-2.png') would have recomputed 'front.png' again, unwinding the disambiguation and
  risking re-triggering the exact collision it had just resolved. Fixed to check by folder
  membership instead of the exact recomputed file name - once a row is in the tenant's current
  folder, later runs leave its name alone.
- Doc-comment inaccuracy: PrintImage.OriginalFileName is the raw client-supplied name (only
  directory-stripped), not the sanitized one - that's StoredFileName. The comment said the
  opposite.
- Dead code: PrintingErrors.PrintImageTenantRequired was defined but unreachable, because
  UploadPrintImageRequest.TenantId was a non-nullable long - a missing tenant id would have hit
  ASP.NET Core's raw model-validation 400 instead of this app's localized ApiResponse envelope.
  Made TenantId nullable and wired the explicit check, matching CreatePrinterRequest/
  CreateProductRequest's established pattern instead of leaving the check dead.
- Invented API caught before use: an early controller draft called a
  ToActionResult(this, conflictOnSuccess: true) overload that does not exist - ResultExtensions.cs
  drives its status-code mapping entirely from ErrorCategory on a failed Result, with no hook for
  forcing a status on a technically-successful one. Rewrote the 409-on-duplicate-upload path to
  construct the ApiResponse/ObjectResult directly instead.
- Two more stale doc comments describing the old auto-replace behavor, on IPrintImageRepo and
  its EF implementation, corrected; the latter also switched from tracked to AsNoTracking, since
  neither of its two current callers (duplicate check in UploadAsync, collision check in
  ReplaceAsync) mutates the row it returns.

Verified: 33/33 error codes have full EN/AR resx parity (script-checked, zero missing, zero
WithArg/{0}-placeholder mismatches); brace/paren balance on every touched file; every
interface-to-implementation-to-controller method signature cross-checked; every new type name
checked for collisions across the whole codebase; both resx files confirmed well-formed XML;
appsettings.json confirmed valid JSON; GenericRepo.GetAllAsync's AsNoTracking + Set.Update's
re-attach behavior confirmed compatible before trusting the migration flow's mutate-after-bulk-read
pattern.
